Ceviri.ekle("en","ekokuma",{
"teknik-tashih-i-sikke-mecidiye-cift-metal":{"baslik": ["The mecidiye and the bimetallic system — why was the currency rebuilt in 1844, and why did it last seventy years?", "f8a41daa"], "metin": ["■ The problem: a currency with a corrupted standard\nFor more than a century the Ottoman kuruş had continuously lost value. According to Şevket Pamuk's calculation in TDV, the pure silver in one kuruş fell from 14.5 grams in 1720 to 2.4 grams in 1824 and to 1 gram in 1844. Over the same period, from the mid-1760s to 1844, food prices in Istanbul rose ten to fifteen times; the fastest increase was between 1808 and 1840. A great many coins of different types were in circulation on the market, some of them debased (mağşûş).\n\n■ DECISION AND IMPLEMENTATION: A CHANGE THAT TOOK FOUR YEARS\nThe new monetary system was launched by a decree dated 26 Safar 1256 (29 April 1840); the equipment needed for the mint was ordered from London. The old coins were definitively withdrawn from circulation on 25 Jumada al-Akhir 1259 (23 July 1843). The first mecidiye was struck on 22 April 1844, the half mecidiye on 18 May and the quarter mecidiye on 19 May 1844. Pamuk calls the operation the 'coinage correction (tashîh-i sikke) of 1844'.\n\n■ WHAT IS BIMETALLISM?\nBoth gold and silver circulating as full-value money, with the ratio between them FIXED by the state. Under the 1844 standard, 1 kuruş contained 1 gram of pure silver and 1 gold lira 6.6 grams of pure gold, and 100 silver kuruş equaled 1 gold lira. The ratio that follows from this: 100 / 6.6 = 15.1 — that is, 1 gram of gold was worth 15.1 grams of silver. In Pamuk's words, the Ottoman state thus passed to a 'bimetallic monetary system defined by a fixed exchange rate'.\n\n■ WHAT IS THE MECIDIYE?\nThe name of the new coins struck in Abdülmecid's name. According to TDV, the name came into general use especially for the 20-kuruş SILVER coin; gold mecidiyes were also struck. By Abdülmecid's death, 15,312,329 gold and 54,987,960 silver mecidiyes had been minted.\n\n■ SIGNIFICANCE: PERMANENCE\nAccording to Pamuk, the real significance of the reform is that the coinage standards adopted, unlike earlier ones, were lasting and could be maintained until 1914: six Ottoman sultans had money struck to the same standard. After a century of debasement (tağşîş, lowering of the standard), this was the first lasting arrangement that stabilized the value of money again.\n\n■ THE END: THE FALL OF SILVER\nThe bimetallic system rested on a fixed ratio; when silver became abundant in the world in the 1870s and its value fell, this ratio could no longer be sustained. In 1879 the Ottoman state also moved to abolish the bimetallic system.", "ae8e3818"], "kisa": ["The silver in one kuruş was 14.5 grams in 1720 and had fallen to 1 gram by 1844. The coinage correction (tashîh-i sikke) halted this slide: it tied the silver kuruş and the gold lira to a fixed ratio — and this standard did not change until 1914.", "a3d5d64b"]},
"teknik-tunel-karakoy-beyoglu-1875":{"baslik": ["Tünel (1875) — the Karaköy-Beyoğlu underground railway", "1515dfc9"], "metin": ["■ Why it was built\nKaraköy (Galata) was the centre of banking and trade, Beyoğlu (Pera) that of entertainment and social life; the Yüksekkaldırım road linked the two. An article from the Faculty of Mechanical Engineering of Istanbul Technical University (İTÜ) relates that the French engineer Eugène-Henri Gavand came to Istanbul as a tourist in 1867 and saw very large numbers of people walking along this road. The same article, relying on Gavand, reports that about 40,000 people walked it daily, and that the road had a gradient of 24% and a width of six metres (in places four metres). Gavand proposed an underground railway in the form of a lift.\n\n■ CONCESSION AND OPENING\nThe project was discussed in the Council of State, and the concession was granted by an imperial decree (irade) dated 10 June 1869; the contract was signed on 6 November 1869 between the Minister of Public Works, Dâvud Pasha, and Gavand. The term of the concession was 42 years, the state's share of the takings 1.5%, with no cash aid from the state for construction. The opening ceremony was held on 17 January 1875; the next day it was opened to the public (Gavand was not at the ceremony; the article attributes this to his sense of grievance). The tunnel was 550.80 m long and the railway within it 626 m; the average journey time was 2.5 minutes and the total cost 4,125,554 francs; the cars were hauled by cable, and the cable system was worked until 1968 by a stationary steam engine at Beyoğlu station. A single ticket cost 2 piastres in 1st class and 1 piastre in 2nd class; in the first 14 days (18-31 January) 75,000 passengers were carried (İTÜ article).\n\n■ WHAT THE TDV SAYS\nA separate article called TÜNEL was `not found` in the TDV (slug HTTP 302). It is mentioned in only two articles, and the two differ on the date: the article on the Beyoğlu Cemetery writes that the Tünel, built by the French engineer Henri Gavand, was opened to service on 17 January 1875, and that during excavation Gavand took care not to damage the cemetery; the article on Galata, however, says that the removal of the graves at the upper end of the cemetery when the Tünel was opened in 1876 became an issue. 17 January 1875 agrees with the date in the article above; 1876 corresponds to the year in which Gavand published his plans in Paris (1876) — this is this site's observation; the TDV does not make this connection.\n\n■ IS IT “THE SECOND OLDEST METRO IN THE WORLD”\nThe İTÜ article describes the Tünel as the second oldest underground urban railway in the world after London's. Such rankings may vary according to the definition of “underground”, “metro” and “funicular”; there is no ranking in the TDV, and the card reports the article's statement.\n\n■ 1869: THE THREE “T”S\nThe article regards 1869 as a turning point in Istanbul's public transport: in the same year concessions were granted for the train, the tramway (Tram) and the Tunnel. This site's interpretation: the Tünel's being mentioned together with the others as one of the new means of transport may be considered a continuation of the line opened by the Şirket-i Hayriyye (1851).", "b02a76e6"], "kisa": ["In 1867 a French tourist saw the crowds walking up the Galata slope and designed an underground railway — the Tünel was opened on 17 January 1875.", "38d9f6e4"]},
"tersane-konferansi-dayatma":{"baslik": ["The Constantinople (Tersane) Conference (1876) — the programme imposed by the great powers", "bdf8c80e"], "metin": ["■ Why the conference met\nIt met to discuss the Balkan crisis, which began with the Herzegovina uprising in 1875 and continued a year later with the Bulgarian uprising and the Ottoman wars with Serbia and Montenegro. The most important phase of the crisis was the Bulgarian uprising, and this question determined the balances at the conference: the news about the suppression of the uprising was turned into political material by the Liberals in Britain through the propaganda of the \"Bulgarian horrors\" and developed into anti-Turkish campaigns. While this paralysed the British government, which favoured the territorial integrity of the Ottoman Empire, it spurred Russia into action.\n\n■ COMING TO THE TABLE\nWhen Ottoman forces routed the Serbian forces on the Morava on 29 October 1876, Russia imposed an unconditional armistice of six weeks or two months through an ultimatum demanding a reply within eight hours. At the same time it proposed that the question be discussed among the Christian powers in the absence of the Ottomans. Seeing that its interests in the East were under threat, Britain proposed on 4 November 1876 that the question be discussed at a conference, and thus regained the lead in finding a solution. The conference met on the upper floor of the Ministry of the Navy building in Kasımpaşa; the first session was held on 23 December 1876.\n\n■ THE MOMENT THE GUNS WERE FIRED\nWhile the delegates were discussing the agenda, the sound of cannon was heard outside: the constitution was being proclaimed. The Ottoman representative told the delegates that these guns announced the constitutional regime and that this regime was the guarantee of the freedoms of Muslims and Christians throughout the Ottoman lands, and then declared that there was no longer any need for such a meeting. The delegates listened in astonishment, but the outcome did not change; the Russian representative Ignatyev said that no importance should be attached to such displays and that they should move on to the agenda, and the conference continued.\n\n■ THE IMPOSED PROGRAMME\nThe subjects to be discussed and the proposals to be presented to the Turkish side had in fact been decided at preparatory meetings that the representatives of the great powers held among themselves at the beginning of December; the decisions agreed on were really the work of the Russian representative Ignatyev and the British representative Salisbury. The programme included the following: Bulgaria would be divided into two provinces, eastern and western, each governed for five years by a Christian governor appointed by the sultan with the consent of the guarantor powers; the governor would be assisted by a provincial assembly; the Turkish army would be concentrated on the frontier and in the principal places, and a national militia and gendarmerie would be established for the province; an international commission would be set up to supervise the reforms, and 5,000 Belgian soldiers would be sent to protect this commission. Bosnia-Herzegovina would be united as a single province but deprived of a militia force. With Serbia and Montenegro the status quo would be taken as the basis, but Serbia would receive Little Zvornik and Montenegro some places in Herzegovina.\n\n■ WHY IT COULD NOT BE ACCEPTED\nThe Ottoman representatives found the programme unacceptable for an independent state; they even stated that they were not authorised to discuss some of its articles. The technical basis of the objection was the constitution: many of the articles presented were contrary to the newly proclaimed Kanun-i Esasi (Basic Law).", "0bb5b411"]},
"tersane-konferansi-sonuc":{"baslik": ["The end of the Constantinople (Tersane) Conference — the assembly chose war", "b2244af6"], "metin": ["■ The final proposal\nAs the Sublime Porte resisted and the conference reached an impasse, the foreign representatives began to discuss softening their proposals. Especially through Midhat Pasha's unofficial contacts and the influence of two British statesmen, the reforms, kept to a minimum, were softened still further and presented on 15 January: the proposal for a European gendarmerie was dropped, and the powers' approval of the appointment of governors was to apply only for the first five years. But the international commission remained. A condition resembling an ultimatum was attached to the proposal: if it was rejected, the conference would end at the session of 18 January and the representatives would return to their countries.\n\n■ 18 JANUARY — THE GENERAL ASSEMBLY\nThe Ottoman side stated that the reply had to be given after taking the votes of all the dignitaries of the realm. On 18 January, under the chairmanship of Midhat Pasha, an assembly of 180 persons met, consisting of the members of the Council of State, the ministers, civil and military dignitaries and non-Muslim religious leaders. The decision was this: it was more befitting the honour of the state and the nation to accept war than to accept unjust proposals that would harm the state and to endure a situation that injured its dignity. In taking this decision, the assembly OPENLY accepted the risk that, if the war brought no benefit, it would face harsher proposals and lose more territory. In other words, the outcome was not unknown; it was a risk consciously accepted.\n\n■ THE COUNTER-PROPOSAL AND THE LAST SESSION\nThe Ottoman side also set out the principles it could accept: two commissions made up of equal numbers of Muslim and Christian members would be established, one to serve for a year for Bosnia-Herzegovina and the other for the Danube and Edirne provinces; they would supervise the implementation of the constitutional reforms and of the measures to be taken, adopt measures to help the people who had suffered harm, and ensure the people's security with the help of the gendarmerie organisation to be established. At the last session on 20 January these proposals were not even made the subject of serious negotiation; the representatives of the great powers rejected them, declared the conference over and left the city, each leaving a chargé d'affaires in his place.\n\n■ THE RESULT: WAR THREE MONTHS LATER\nWith the break-up of the conference, the door to the Ottoman-Russian war was opened. Russia declared war on 24 April 1877. The treaties to be signed a year later were far harsher than the proposals rejected at the conference: Serbia, Montenegro and Romania became independent, Bulgaria was established, Kars-Ardahan-Batum passed to Russia and the administration of Cyprus to Britain, and Bosnia-Herzegovina was left to Austrian occupation. The possibility the assembly had risked came about exactly.\n\n■ A SIDE RESULT\nThe conference also revealed a deep crack in Britain's Eastern policy: the chief British representative, Salisbury, was working in harmony with the Russian representative, and the other British representative accused him of serving Russian designs, the greatest threat to British interests. How fragile the Ottoman calculation of leaning on Britain in 1878 was had already appeared at this table, before the war even began.", "1f4f7ab4"]},
"timar-sistemi-1432-1827":{"sebep.b": ["A state whose money economy and central treasury were not sufficiently developed, and whose army consisted largely of cavalry, maintaining its soldiers on the taxes of the land", "2e012c26"], "sonuc.b": ["The conversion in 1827 of about 5,200 timariot sipahis from 53 sanjaks in Rumelia and Anatolia into cavalry of the Asâkir-i Mansûre, and the timar regime's entry on the path to liquidation", "b221aa79"], "bag": ["Why does '1432' in the chronology carry no day or month? Because the 'institutionalisation' of the timar was not a day but a process. According to the TDV article on the timar, the first documentary reference to the system goes back to the reign of Orhan Bey; 1432 is not the year in which the system was born, but the year of the oldest surviving summary (icmal) register of timars — the register of the sanjak of Arvanid in Albania, dated AH 835. The article says that this register shows that all the basic principles of the system had already taken shape by then; in other words, 1432 is not a beginning but the first photograph we have. Because the year AH 835 falls between September 1431 and August 1432, the TDV article on the cebelü refers to the same register as 'dated 1431' — the two articles do not contradict each other; they are reading the two ends of the same Hijri year. That is why no source gives a day or month, and none has been invented.", "8341973e"], "metin": ["What a timar is. According to the TDV article on the timar, the word means 'care, attention' in the dictionary, and in Turkish it is synonymous with dirlik (livelihood grant). As a term, it is a non-hereditary allocation made in the central provinces to support a cavalry force and the military-administrative hierarchy. The sipahi was given not ownership of the land but the right to collect specified taxes from specified villages. The article links the roots of the system to several traditions: the Byzantine pronoia (the word timar is the exact equivalent of the Greek pronoia), the iqta of the Islamic states, and Turco-Mongol tradition (terms such as 'cebe' are Mongolian).\n\nDIRLIK, timar, zeamet, has — the difference lies in the size of the revenue. Dirlik is the umbrella term; beneath it there are three grades according to size. The TDV article on the zeamet, citing the Kanunname of Ali Çavuş, draws the line precisely: a dirlik one akçe short of 20,000 akçes is a timar; one between 20,000 and 100,000 akçes is a zeamet, and its holder is called a zaim; one above 100,000 akçes is a has and is reserved for sanjak governors, beylerbeys and viziers (those belonging to the sultan are the 'imperial has', havâss-ı hümâyun). But this threefold division, too, became established only later: according to the TDV article on the timar, in the Arvanid register of 1432 the dirliks fall into only two classes, 'has' and 'timar'; according to the article on the zeamet, the word 'zeamet' never occurs in the registers of the reign of Murad II (the term zaim is used), whereas from the reign of the Conqueror onwards it appears frequently in the tax registers.\n\nThe SIPAHI's obligations. The timariot sipahi was obliged to be present on campaign; according to the TDV, when the campaigning season began (generally in March-April), the sipahis were assembled through the çeribaşı, the subaşı and the sanjak governor, and the beylerbey inspected them and led them to the imperial army. In peacetime the sipahi collected from the peasant the taxes fixed by law (öşür or tithe, çift resmi, ispenç) and could not take more; in the words of the TDV, the Ottoman peasant was in this sense free, and the sipahi could not make him work at his whim. He had the right to bring back a peasant who had fled within ten (later fifteen) years.\n\nThe CEBELÜ reckoning. According to the TDV article on the cebelü, cebelü means 'an armoured, fully equipped soldier', and their number depended directly on the revenue of the timar. The first examples are seen in the Arvanid register: a sipahi with an income below 1,000 akçes was 'himself the cebelü', that is, he went on campaign alone in his armour; with 1,500-2,000 akçes he brought a gulâm (retainer) with him; holders of larger timars took part with the better armour called 'bürüme' and with cebelüs according to their income. The Kanunname of the Conqueror standardised this: a graduated scale up to 15,000 akçes, and, for timars exceeding 15,000, one cebelü for every 3,000 akçes. The TDV article on the timar writes that holders of zeamets and has brought one cebelü for every 5,000 akçes. Two notes on the figures: İdris-i Bitlisi estimates the timariot army of Anatolia in 1473 at about 20,000 and that of Rumelia at about 24,000 (according to the TDV, the cebelüs must be included in this number); the official records of 1527-1528 give 37,521 timar holders, of whom 9,563 were fortress guards.\n\nGRANT and withdrawal. A timar was entered in the summary register as an indivisible unit called a 'kılıç' (sword); the document of assignment (tahvil tezkiresi) was prepared at the centre, and a berat (diploma) was issued. As a rule, a member of the reaya could not become a sipahi; but according to the TDV, volunteers, akıncıs (raiders) and fortress soldiers who distinguished themselves on campaign could receive a timar on recommendation, and during the long campaigns of 1578-1618 thousands of Anatolians entered the sipahi class in this way. Being a Muslim was not a requirement: in some sanjaks the proportion of Christian timar holders reached 35%. The chief reason for the withdrawal of a timar was failure to take part in a campaign; a serious crime or oppression of the reaya would also cause the timar to be lost. If a sipahi whose timar had been taken away (mazul) did not take part in a campaign within seven years, he lost his status as a sipahi altogether and became a tax-paying member of the reaya. For this reason the article stresses that sipahi status was 'not hereditary in the full sense'. There were also exceptions: the yurtluk-ocaklık holdings in Eastern Anatolia and, from the 17th century, the ocaklık timars in Bosnia could be inherited within the family.\n\nDISSOLUTION. The TDV article on the timar describes the dissolution as the combined effect of several forces: in the Austrian war of 1593-1606 the need for infantry armed with muskets made salaried soldiers necessary, and on the battlefield the sipahi was displaced by the Janissaries, the sekban and the saruca; after 1584, while timar revenues remained fixed, inflation reached 100%, and the sipahi could no longer go on long campaigns; the expulsion from the profession of the Anatolian sipahis who did not take part in the campaign of 1596 caused great turmoil, and those dismissed or left without timars joined the Celali bands; the government diverted timar revenues to the treasury and the palace circle — in the campaign of 1621, 2,882 çavuş, 681 müteferrika and 522 clerks were timar holders. At the Erzurum muster of 1715, 2,119 timars passed to the treasury; the regulations of 1777 and 1791 attempted reform, but without result.\n\nTHE END. According to the TDV, in 1827 about 5,200 timariot sipahis from fifty-three sanjaks in Rumelia and Anatolia were converted, together with their timar revenues, into cavalry of the Asâkir-i Mansûre; since the revenues were not enough for their livelihood, some had their timars taken away and were put on salaries, and it was envisaged that the timars would be liquidated on the death of the last sipahi. Local problems connected with timar land were handed down to the Republic. Like its beginning, its end cannot be tied to a single day and a single city: the entry for 1827 in the chronology sums up in a single line a transformation spread across fifty-three sanjaks.", "857ec8d9"]},
"topkapi-sarayi-insasi":{"oneri": ["The palace was not a single building but a chain of SECRECY increasing from the outside inwards", "9725eca1"], "sebep.b": ["The need, after the conquest of Istanbul, to establish in the new capital a centre of government that, in terms of security and symbolic legitimacy, dominated the city, was open to the sea and was separate from the old Byzantine settlement", "1444bb4a"], "sonuc.b": ["The completion of the New Palace on Seraglio Point (today's Topkapı Palace) and the move of the centre of the state there from the first (Old) palace at Bayezid", "22ee3a7b"], "bag": ["According to the TDV Encyclopaedia's article on Topkapı Palace, the palace was built on a site 'dominating the city from every side', so as to look out over the Marmara, Üsküdar and the Golden Horn. The date 883 (1478) above the Imperial Gate (Bab-ı Hümayun) indicates its completion; construction had probably begun in 1465. To distinguish it from the first palace at Beyazıt, it was referred to as 'Saray-ı Cedid-i Amire' (the New Imperial Palace) — the name 'Topkapı' was borrowed much later from another pavilion on the shore.", "4f605ec7"], "metin": ["The palace was an organisational chain made up of three successive gates and the courtyards they separated. The Birun (outer service) comprised units such as the guards, stables and kitchens in the first courtyard and partly in the second. In the second courtyard, beyond the Gate of Salutation (Bab-üs Selam), affairs of state were discussed in the domed Imperial Council (Divan-ı Hümayun). The third courtyard, behind the Gate of Felicity (Bab-üs Saade), was reserved for the Enderun, that is, the palace school where devshirme youths were educated and prepared for the ranks of state service. Innermost of all was the Harem, the private living quarters of the sultan's family. This seclusion, increasing from the outside inwards, was also a hierarchy: the public could enter the first courtyard, only those coming on state business were admitted to the second, and only a very narrow circle was allowed into the third courtyard and the Harem — the architecture of the palace was the centralised and stratified order of the state cast in stone.", "6dcf29aa"]},
"tosun-pasa-film-mi-tarih-mi":{"baslik": ["Is the Tosun Paşa played by Kemal Sunal this Tosun Pasha? — where popular memory and the sources part ways", "a36e7b32"], "metin": ["The question belongs to the curious reader; the standard belongs to the source. This card does not use a film as a historical source, and the film was not watched; the plot is taken solely from the description in a peer-reviewed academic article. The reason is a rule: the atlas's source discipline is not relaxed, not even for a comedy.\n\nWhat the FILM tells (the academic description). The study by Muhsine Sekmen and Gizem Kul Kalkandelen, published in Erciyes Akademi, summarises the film as follows: “The film Tosun Paşa (Tibet, 1976) deals with the struggle for a district called Yeşil Vadi (Green Valley) between two long-established families in Alexandria in the Ottoman period: the Tellioğulları and the Seferoğulları.” The setup is this: “Daver Bey, the governor of Alexandria, has a young and beautiful daughter named Leyla. Each of the two families wishes to take Leyla as a bride in order to gain possession of Yeşil Vadi. By passing off Şaban, a servant of the house, as Tosun Pasha, one of the influential figures of the Cairo palace, the Tellioğulları seize an opportunity to gain Yeşil Vadi and Leyla. But when the real Tosun Pasha appears, things do not go as they had planned.”\n\nTHREE measurements, three separate answers.\n① Is it the same person? No — the two descriptions do not match. The film's Tosun Pasha is one of “the influential figures of the Cairo palace”, placed in the position of arbiter in a land dispute; his name works less as an office than as a figure defined by a title, and his dramatic function is to be “a high-ranking personage who can be impersonated”. The historical Ahmed Tosun Pasha, by contrast, is, in the words of the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the man who was “appointed governor of Jeddah and commander of the army to be sent against the Wahhabis” — the son of the governor of Egypt, head of the army that set out from Egypt to the Hijaz in September 1811, a soldier who died of the plague in 1816. Not a palace dignitary arbitrating a lawsuit over land.\n② Is the event in the film historically accurate? The question cannot be answered as it stands, because what the film narrates is not a historical event: the academic description names neither a year nor an event, and the only frame is “the Ottoman period”. There is no date to test; the only things that can be tested are the names, and they are absent from the sources — neither an Egyptian household called “Tellioğulları” or “Seferoğulları”, nor a governor called “Daver Bey”, nor a district called “Yeşil Vadi” appears in the sources consulted. During the historical Tosun Pasha's years in Egypt (1811-1816), the governor of Egypt was his father, Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala. ⇒ The correct statement is this: the film does not tell history wrongly; it does not tell history.\n③ Is there any link between them? This was checked and none was found: none of the academic sources opened connects the film's character with the historical Tosun Pasha; no source was found that says “it was inspired by him”, nor one that says “it has nothing to do with him”. This text does not fill that gap with guesswork.\n\nIts place in CULTURAL life — the measurable part. What the card can say about the film's weight in Turkey is limited to what can be tied to figures. ① The director's first film: the study of Kartal Tibet's cinema by Yunus Namaz and Ayşegül Şahin states that “The first film made by the director Tibet is Tosun Paşa, produced in 1976, and his last film is the work entitled Hayat Güzeldir, produced in 2008. Between 1976 and 2008 Tibet directed a total of 67 films” — in other words, it was the opening film of a directing career of 67 films. ② Audience rating: in Yavuz Ağca's study describing Turkish cinema through web mining, “Tosun Paşa (1976, p. = 9.0)” appears among the highest audience ratings; the study lists this rating after that of Hababam Sınıfı (9.3). (This is a measurement of audience ratings, not an artistic judgement.) ③ The birth of the duo: the history of comedy cinema by Selda Solak and Yavuz Bayram records that Şener Şen “also acted together with Sunal in films such as ‘Süt Kardeşler’ (1976), ‘Tosun Paşa’ (1976), ‘Kibar Feyzo’ (1978), ‘Çöpçüler Kralı’ (1978) and ‘Davaro’ (1981)” and describes the pattern: “In the films in which he forms a duo with Kemal Sunal, Şen portrays the two-faced, cunning and fraudulent character, while Sunal portrays naive, well-meaning, clean-hearted characters.”\n\nAND the same academic source also offers a criticism. The study that describes the film does not do so in order to praise it: examining the nanny character in the film (Yasemin Esmergül), Sekmen and Kul Kalkandelen say that “in this film, too, Esmergül has very few scenes and lines, and she makes no contribution to the world of the film or to the development of the hero”; in the film the actress “is given no name other than that of the nanny.” The study's general verdict on the seven films it covers is harsher: “The use of negative expressions such as ‘coal bin, discoloured woman, charcoal-black woman’ for Afro-Turkish performers shows the discrimination applied to them on the basis of their skin colour” and “expressions of humiliation are presented as natural and as an element of comedy.” ⇒ A film's place in culture is measured not only by how much it is loved, but also by what it normalises.\n\nCONCLUSION. In popular memory, “Tosun Paşa” evokes not a solemn send-off but a burst of laughter; in the sources, it is the name of a young man at the head of the army that retook the Two Holy Places (Haramayn), whom his commanders regarded “as a child”, and who died of the plague five years later. This is exactly where the work of this atlas becomes visible: the chronology entry shows September 1811, the map draws what changed in the Hijaz on that date — and both together tell of a different human being from the namesake on the cinema screen.", "42380f8c"], "kisa": ["In Turkey the name “Tosun Paşa” calls to mind not a commander but a comedy. The card sets two accounts side by side: the film's Tosun Pasha is, in the academic description, one of “the influential figures of the Cairo palace” and the arbiter in a lawsuit over land; history's Ahmed Tosun Pasha is the son of the governor of Egypt, governor of Jeddah and commander of the Hijaz army. The same name, two different people — one of them fictional.", "4f900341"]},
"tosun-pasa-kimdir":{"baslik": ["Who was Tosun Pasha — his father brought him onto the stage in the same stroke as the Mamluk danger", "c7d333b5"], "metin": ["Biographical details — and why they are scattered. There is no article called \"Tosun Pasha\" in the TDV (the measurement of the search is in this card's internal note); the biography was compiled from inside five separate articles. His full name: İlhan Şahin's article `abbas-hilmi-i`, in introducing Abbas Hilmi, governor of Egypt, says that he \"is the son of Ahmed Tosun Pasha\". Mustafa L. Bilge's article `diriye` gives the assignment of duties: \"Mehmed Ali Pasha sent one of his sons, Tosun Ahmed Pasha, to the Jeddah and Taif districts, and after his death sent İbrahim Pasha to the Medina and northern Arabia districts.\" And the place that gives both offices in a single sentence is, strangely, the sentence about a massacre — `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa`: \"At the great feast he gave on 1 March 1811 in honour of his son Tosun, who had been appointed governor of Jeddah and commander of the army to be sent against the Wahhabis, he had the Mamluk beys, with a few exceptions, killed.\" ⇒ Tosun Pasha enters history with two offices and a banquet.\n\nWHY him, why not his father. Kamuran Şimşek's study states the reason openly: Mehmed Ali, \"in order to remove the possibility of a rising breaking out behind his back, did not go on the campaign himself but placed his son Tosun Pasha at the head of the army.\" The same article also says that the campaign was part of the same plan as the Mamluk question: the army had been assembled \"in order to remove completely the Mamluk danger in Egypt\"; \"it was announced through town criers that Tosun Pasha would set out on the Hejaz Campaign on 1 March, and a feast was held to celebrate this campaign.\" The centre also consented: \"Mehmet Ali Pasha's assigning his son Tosun to settle the Wahhabi question was received favourably by the Sublime Porte.\"\n\nBY SEA or by land — both. The source's answer to a question that comes to mind about the map stands in a single sentence (Şimşek): \"In this campaign, which began in September 1811, the infantry travelled by sea aboard ships, while the cavalry unit travelled by land.\" Kevser Değirmenci gives the composition and strength of the army: \"a force of 3,500 men, mostly Albanians and Turks, with some regular soldiers and infantry trained by Frenchmen.\" The objective was a port town, and why it was that town is written in the TDV's article `yenbu`: Yanbu is \"the port of Medina\", and the Wahhabis, \"cutting the link between Medina's grain store and Yanbu\", had besieged the town. The same article records the outcome with year precision: \"Under the leadership of Tosun and İbrahim pashas, sons of Mehmed Ali Pasha of Kavala, governor of Egypt, Yanbu was brought back under Ottoman rule (1811).\" Şimşek also gives the month: \"The Egyptian forces seized Yanbu in October and used it as a base for the operations to be carried out.\" ⇒ The campaign began in September, the base was established in October. (For the atlas's day for this entry, see the internal note.)\n\nA commander looked on as a CHILD — and the first rout. Tosun did not want to wait; in a letter he wrote to his father he said that \"after taking control of Yanbu, he preferred to take action instead of waiting for the reinforcements to come from Egypt\" (Şimşek). The attack on 23 November 1811 on the entrenchments in the foothills ended in a rout. The cause comes to the same point in both sources. Şimşek: \"The reason for the defeat of the army led by Tosun Pasha arose from the soldiers' failure to obey the orders given by their commanders.\" Değirmenci says the same thing more bluntly: \"This defeat arose from the commanders' looking upon Tosun Pasha as a child and not carrying out his orders.\" Most of the defeated returned to Egypt in 1812 and pointed to \"one another as the cause of the defeat\"; Mehmed Ali punished those responsible.\n\nTHE RETURN: Medina, Mecca, Taif. Reinforcements arrived, and the forces of the Sharif of Mecca, Sharif Ghalib, and of the Harb Bedouin who opposed the Wahhabis joined them. Medina was besieged for two weeks; battering the city with cannon \"was not deemed fitting.\" The news of the conquest reached Egypt on 15 December, and Süleyman Pasha, governor of Damascus, also reported in an official letter that \"Tosun Pasha had conquered Medina and succeeded in driving the Wahhabis out of these places\" (Şimşek). Then came Jeddah, and after it Mecca and Taif. The reward was concrete: \"The recovery of the holy places was greeted with satisfaction by the Ottoman State, and after this victory Ahmet Tosun Pasha was granted the Province of Habesh, the Sanjak of Jeddah and the office of Shaykh al-Haram of Mecca, with the rank of vizier.\" Mahmud II, \"upon the capture of Medina, Mecca and Taif one after another, sent Mehmet Ali Pasha and his son Tosun Pasha swords, kaftans and robes of honour.\"\n\nPEACE — and the father's refusal to recognise that peace. In January 1815, after the great battle he won against Faisal b. Saud at Bisl, east of Taif, Tosun Pasha brought Abdullah b. Saud to the negotiating table; according to Şimşek, by the agreement \"Najd and Qasim were left to the Wahhabis, and the Hejaz to the Ottomans.\" Değirmenci puts the treaty in June 1815 and tells what followed: \"Mehmet Ali Pasha rejected the terms of the Wahhabi leader; as a result the treaty made by Tosun Pasha was left void, and both sides had begun preparations for war.\" The TDV's article `abdullah-b-suud` (Feridun Emecen) gives the same verdict: \"During the Najd operation of Tosun Pasha, son of Mehmed Ali Pasha, he made a truce with him; however, the peace negotiations produced no result.\"\n\nDEATH — not at the front but back home. Emecen ends the same sentence thus: \"When Tosun Pasha died in 1816 and his brother İbrahim Pasha came to Arabia in his place, the struggle intensified.\" Şimşek writes the cause: \"Upon Tosun Pasha's losing his life from plague in 1816, Mehmet Ali Pasha's elder son İbrahim Pasha took his place\" — and the detail is even more touching: Mehmed Ali reported to the centre that, after the wars in the Hejaz, \"Tosun Pasha, who was in the Rosetta district of Egypt for a change of air and some rest, fell ill and lost his life the next day.\" The provinces of Jeddah and Habesh were given to his brother İbrahim; İbrahim Pasha set out on 23 September 1816, and the citadel of Diriyah fell on 15 September 1818. One of İbrahim's first acts is telling: he \"made efforts to improve the health conditions that had been one of the chief causes of the difficulties his brother Tosun had suffered.\"\n\nAND the line continued. The name of this young commander, who was on the stage for barely five years, remained in the dynasty: one of the last lines of the article `kavalali-mehmed-ali-pasa` introduces the governor who took Mehmed Ali's place in 1848 — \"Tosun's son Abbas Hilmi Pasha took his place.\"", "2ff2fb6d"], "kisa": ["His full name was Ahmed Tosun Pasha: son of Mehmed Ali of Kavala, governor of Jeddah and commander of the Hejaz army. Because his commanders \"looked upon him as a child\", he was routed in his first campaign; he then recovered Medina, Mecca and Taif, and died of plague in 1816. His son Abbas Hilmi became governor of Egypt in 1848.", "831790fe"]},
"varvar-ali-pasa-perihan-hanim-1648":{"soru": ["Which orders did Varvar Ali Pasha refuse before rebelling?", "b228f039"], "baslik": ["The pasha who would not send a woman to the palace died at the hands of her husband", "4f8ec1f0"], "metin": ["Varvar Ali Pasha, who as a Bosnian village boy was taken in the devshirme, rose in the palace through falconry and told the story of his life in a verse work of his own, received two orders from Istanbul in the autumn of 1647, while he was beylerbeyi of Sivas. The first demanded 30,000 kuruş under the name of 'holiday pocket money'; the pasha had the revenue of the province calculated and reported that this sum could not be paid. The second was that Perihan Hanım, the lawful wife in Sivas of İpşir Mustafa Pasha, beylerbeyi of Anatolia, should be sent to Istanbul to be presented to the sultan; according to Kâtib Çelebi, the pasha rejected this too. He was dismissed, did not let those sent to kill him into the city, proclaimed that the affairs of state were left in the hands of women, and decided to march on Istanbul. Evliya Çelebi claims that some circles in the capital, even Kösem Sultan, encouraged him with letters. Having defeated and captured Köprülü Mehmed Pasha, Varvar Ali was surprised on 20 May 1648 in a raid by İpşir Mustafa Pasha, whom he thought had come to join him; the man whose wife he had refused to send to the palace had him seized and executed.", "f9b087d7"], "not": ["The secret letters of support from Istanbul appear only in Evliya Çelebi's account.", "abc67e71"]},
"yavuz-baba-zehir-soylentisi":{"soru": ["Did Selim the Grim have his father poisoned?", "8b655700"], "baslik": ["The suspicious death of the deposed Bayezid II", "e96748d4"], "metin": ["Under pressure from the Janissaries, Bayezid II left the throne to his son Selim on 24 April 1512 and departed from Istanbul to go to Didymoteicho (Dimetoka). According to the TDV article on Bayezid, he was taken ill on the way, in the village of Abalar near Çorlu, and died on 10 June 1512; the encyclopaedia finds the cause of death extremely suspicious and notes that the possibility of poisoning, based on certain local and foreign records, is debated. The same encyclopaedia's article on Selim the Grim, however, states that the rumours that Selim had his father poisoned appear especially in Western sources but could not be confirmed by other sources.", "dbb08b2a"], "not": ["The sources differ in emphasis: one leaves open the possibility of poisoning, while the other does not regard the attribution of it to Selim as supported by other sources. The card takes no side.", "2ea6cd5f"]},
"yavuz-olum-ur":{"soru": ["Which illness killed Selim the Grim?", "67505564"], "baslik": ["The tumour on his back: the last two months of Selim the Grim", "1ca2abb6"], "metin": ["In the summer of 1520 Selim I set out from Istanbul towards Edirne; the TDV considers it highly probable that this journey was connected with his failing health and the plague epidemic in the city. Because of a large tumour that appeared on his back, he could not go beyond Çorlu; two months of treatment brought no result, and he died on the night of 21–22 September 1520. The encyclopaedia writes that the tumour was 'probably a plague bubo'; that is, the diagnosis is a possibility, not a certainty. According to what his close attendant Hasan Can told his son Hoca Sâdeddin, the sultan drew his last breath while repeating the Sura Yasin that Hasan Can was reciting.", "a3bf4938"], "not": ["The account of the final moments rests on a single witness, Hasan Can, and has reached us through his son's work.", "00bb0c1e"]},
"yemen-elden-cikis-geri-donus-ve-birinci-dunya-savasi":{"ad": ["Yemen: the Ottomans who entered twice and twice tried to hold on", "45614a7f"], "metin": ["The first entry and exit (briefly). The Ottomans' interest in Yemen arose at the beginning of the sixteenth century from the Portuguese danger in the Red Sea and the Indian Ocean. Zabid was taken on 20 June 1516; on his Indian expedition of 1538-39 Hadım Süleyman Pasha put Aden and Zabid in order and laid the foundation of the beylerbeylik of Yemen (27 February 1539); the resistance of the Zaydis in the mountainous interior continued until Sanaa was taken in 1547 and, after 1568, retaken in Sinan Pasha's second conquest (1569-1571). In the face of the advance of the Zaydi imams (the Qasimis) between 1626 and 1635, the Ottomans gave up first Sanaa (1629), then Zabid and Mocha; the last Ottoman forces left Mocha on 22 October 1635. The details are written on the card 'dunya-yemende-osmanli-hakimiyeti'. TDV writes that even after 1635 some administrators and soldiers remained in Yemen, that the coffee trade continued and that there was an attempt to re-establish rule in 1672, but that an expedition was abandoned in 1694, and that one cannot speak of an Ottoman presence in Yemen until the beginning of the nineteenth century.\n\nTHE SECOND coming (nineteenth century). (1) 1833: a bey named Türkçe Bilmez, who had rebelled against Mehmed Ali Pasha, entered Yemen with soldiers he had gathered from Jeddah and took several towns but could not hold on. (2) 1835: Mehmed Ali Pasha sent an army and naval force under the command of Emin Bey and took al-Luhayyah and Hodeidah; İbrahim Pasha's new forces seized the Tihama. With the departure of the Egyptian army, Hodeidah passed in 1840 into the hands of Sharif Husayn, to whom the Tihama was surrendered (the TDV article 'Tihâme' speaks for the same year of 'the control of the Saudis'; the two articles diverge on this point). (3) 1839: Britain occupied Aden; the Ottoman attempt to bring Yemen under control again falls in the same period. (4) 1849: the Sharif of Mecca Ibn Awn and Tevfik Pasha the Cypriot landed by sea on Kamaran Island and at Hodeidah with a force of about 3000 men, and the town was surrendered; they wanted to take Sanaa, but because of a popular uprising they were forced to return to Hodeidah. The Tihama was taken back by the Ottomans in 1849. When Tevfik Pasha died in 1850, the part of Yemen in Ottoman hands began to be administered by governors or district governors (mutasarrıf); efforts were made to suppress the revolts around Hodeidah and Asir. (5) 1871: Ahmed Muhtar Pasha suppressed the revolt in Asir and took Sanaa, re-establishing state authority, and made Yemen a vilayet; in 1872 the sanjaks of Asir, Hodeidah and Taiz were formed, with Sanaa as the centre. The Seventh Army, set up in Yemen in 1869, had 25,000 soldiers.\n\nREVOLTS and Imam Yahya. The order secured by Ahmed Muhtar Pasha lasted about twenty years; in 1889 the Zaydis revolted because of the inadequacy of Ottoman officials and administrative injustice. A new uprising was suppressed in 1895-1897. In the revolt begun by Imam Yahya Hamid al-Din in 1902, the Governor Faik Pasha was besieged in Sanaa; when the revolt intensified in 1905 and famine began in besieged Sanaa, the city had to be surrendered to the rebels; afterwards Ahmed Feyzi Pasha, in an operation between 15 July and 1 September 1905, entered Sanaa and removed the tribal chiefs. When the revolt of Sheikh Idrisi in Asir in 1909 combined with the revolt of Imam Yahya in 1911, Ahmed İzzet Pasha was sent to Yemen on 28 February 1911 aboard the cruiser Hamidiye; with an army of about 50,000 men drawn from the Seventh Army and from Istanbul, he raised the siege of Sanaa. The agreement made with Imam Yahya on 13 October 1911 (22 open and 5 secret articles) left to the imam the administration of the mountain regions of the Zaydis, including Sanaa; the imam would remain in his position without the right to make treaties with foreign states, would give up the claim to be 'amir al-mu'minin' (commander of the faithful) and would receive 20,000 Ottoman gold liras a year.\n\nITS IMPORTANCE for the Ottomans. TDV records only the source's own sentence: in the sixteenth century the justification of the Ottoman presence was not 'tahsîl-i mâl' (collecting revenue) but was directed at the 'protection (sıyânet) of the Noble Kaaba' (document: BA, MD, nr. 7, hk. 2738). Because Yemen lay at the junction of trade routes, first Portugal and the Netherlands, and then from the nineteenth century Britain, France and Italy took an interest in it: Britain took Aden in 1839, supported the Idrisi revolt in Asir in 1912 and in 1913 concluded protection agreements with the Nine Cantons (Nawahi-i Tis'a) sheikhdoms around Aden; France tried to buy Kamaran Island in 1851 and occupied the island of Shaykh Said in 1865; Italy came to the region in 1905 and in 1911 bombarded al-Qunfudhah, Hodeidah and Shaykh Said. With their armies in Yemen the Ottomans prevented the colonial powers from entering Yemen (TDV's assessment).\n\nTHE FIRST World War question. TDV has no direct answer to the question 'Why was there fighting in Yemen while they could not protect their own core lands?'; but the article gives these facts: the peace secured by the 1911 agreement lasted throughout the war; Imam Yahya helped in meeting the needs of the Ottoman army in Yemen; the soldiers of the 7th Corps in Sanaa undertook operations against the Idrisi forces in Asir and against the British base at Aden; Imam Yahya contributed soldiers to these operations; British ships bombarded the ports of Hodeidah and al-Luhayyah from time to time. After the Armistice of Mudros, the commander at Taiz, Ali Said Pasha, surrendered at Aden with the 39th Division; thanks to the fact that Galib Bey, commander of the 40th Division in the Tihama, acted together with Ahmed Tevfik Pasha, the heavy weapons and ammunition were left to Imam Yahya. On 5 March 1919 the contingent in Yemen, consisting of 3883 soldiers and civil officials, surrendered to the British at Hodeidah. The last governor, Mahmud Nedim Bey, left Yemen in May 1924; legally, Yemen ceased to be Ottoman territory as a result of the Treaty of Lausanne.\n\nTHIS PAGE'S interpretation. A reading that can be drawn from TDV's facts: the Ottoman armed force in Yemen was not a new build-up dispatched with the First World War but a force that had been there since 1869 (the Seventh Army) and the expedition of 1911; this force took part in a regional war against the British base at Aden and the Idrisi force in Asir. As for the 'why was Yemen defended' part of the question (the security of the Hejaz and the pilgrimage route, control of the Red Sea), this is not a sourced answer but an interpretation made as the continuation, centuries later, of the justification of 'protecting the Kaaba' mentioned above.", "f1c7cdce"], "kisa": ["Yemen, effectively abandoned in 1635, was regained in the nineteenth century through three separate attempts (1835, 1849, 1871-72); it remained in hand until the end of the First World War.", "fcb43b60"], "not": ["The 'return to the Tihama coast' entry to which this card is linked in the chronology lists Hodeidah, Zabid and Moha; the TDV articles on Yemen and Hodeidah give for 1849 only Kamaran Island and Hodeidah, and the capture of Zabid and Mocha in the same year does not appear in these articles.", "5a526205"], "bag": ["On the first conquest of Yemen and the reasons it could not be held see the card 'dunya-yemende-osmanli-hakimiyeti'; on Mehmed Ali Pasha's Arabian campaign see the Egyptian campaign cards.", "5a93bd2d"]},
"yeniceri-ocagi-kurulusu":{"sebep.b": ["The need of the principality's army, from Orhan Gazi onward, with the crossing into Rumelia and the growing conquests, for a standing, salaried infantry force tied to the center", "cfd3ee6b"], "sonuc.b": ["With the accession of Murad I, the establishment of the pençik system and of the Novice Corps (Acemi Ocağı) at Gallipoli — the nucleus of the Janissary organization", "88edaafc"], "bag": ["According to the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam, the idea of taking one-fifth (pençik) of the prisoners of war, training them and adding them to the standing army matured gradually between the end of Orhan's reign and the time of Murad I; historians still debate the exact moment of foundation, and some point to the reign of Bayezid I the Thunderbolt.", "6ddd1be6"], "metin": ["The Janissaries were the infantry branch of the kapıkulu (the sultan's salaried household troops): youths recruited through the devshirme and pençik systems were trained in the Novice Corps and then enrolled in companies called 'orta'. The number of ortas reached 165 in the reign of Suleiman the Lawgiver and later 196; the corps was organized with the ranks of çorbacı and agha and was an institution receiving a regular salary (ulufe) from the center. Mahmud II abolished the corps by turning cannon on it in the Auspicious Incident (Vak'a-i Hayriyye) of 15-16 June 1826.", "b53f43ad"]},
"yenilesme-karsiti-ayaklanmalar-etiket":{"baslik": ["“Uprisings of Those Who Did Not Want Innovation” — What the Sources Say in Five Cases", "ff766e99"], "metin": ["In Ottoman history the uprisings that broke out right after a move toward modernization form a series, and they are often told with a single formula: a rising by those opposed to innovation. Below, five cases are listed with their reasons as far as the source states them; let the reader weigh for himself or herself how far the formula holds in each case. (The scope of this card is internal to the Ottoman state; for a world comparison see the card `kiyas-yenilige-tepki-ayaklanmalari`, and for a typology of the depositions of sultans the card `tartisma-osmanli-darbeleri-tipoloji` — neither has been repeated here.)\n\n① 1622 · The Incident of Young Osman (Osman II). The revolt began on 8 Rajab 1031 / 19 May 1622, and the sultan was strangled at Yedikule the next day. The trigger was the preparation the sultan was making to go on pilgrimage: a rumor spread that this preparation was a plan to gather troops from Anatolia and do away with the Janissaries. Here the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam enters an important caveat: the reports that the sultan had given up on Istanbul, that he would march against the Janissaries with troops from Damascus and Egypt and that he would move the capital are “the propaganda of those opposed to him, which is reflected even in the sources of the period”; the account of Hüseyin Tuği, who narrated the event through the eyes of the kapıkulu troops hostile to Osman II, passed into later chronicles and led modern historiography to regard him as a ruler who was “a reformer, an innovator, wishing to carry out wide-ranging reforms”. In other words, both the portrait of the “innovative sultan” and that of a “revolt against innovation” may have been fed by the same hostile source. And the reason for the ulema's resentment was not reform but the cutting off of their arpalık (stipendiary revenue assignments).\n\n② 1730 · The Patrona Halil revolt. It began in Beyazıt on 12 Rabi al-Awwal 1143 / 25 September 1730; on the night of 1 October Damad Ibrahim Pasha was surrendered for execution, and on 2 October Ahmed III gave up the throne. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam's list of causes is long, and innovation is not at the head of it: a political, economic and social reaction going back to Passarowitz, the loss of Belgrade and Temeşvar, the failure suffered in Iran, the burden of the imdadiyye (emergency aid) taxes, the granting of mukataas (revenue farms) for life, the tax burden on the artisans' guilds, and the luxurious spending of the statesmen, verging on extravagance. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam states explicitly that activities such as the opening of the printing press, the translation committee, the libraries, tile-making, the paper and textile workshops and the fire brigade “did not present a situation of concern to the broad masses of the people”; what attracted attention was the pavilions in the French architectural style, the luxury and the dissipation. Moreover, the preparation of the revolt had been undertaken by the grand vizier's own son-in-law, the kaymakam Mustafa Pasha, who had his eye on office.\n\n③ 1807 · The Kabakçı revolt. This is the case in the series that best fits the formula of “opposition to modernization”: the spark is directly the Nizam-i Cedid — the bringing of the yamaks into this system and the incitement that they would be made to wear Nizam-i Cedid uniforms (TDV `kabakci-isyani`). Yet even here the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam stand behind the scene of the revolt; for details see the card `kabakci-isyani-niye-cikti`.\n\n④ 1859 · The Kuleli Incident. A conspiratorial society that was broken up on 14 September 1859, through an informer, before it had even gone into action. The causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam are: the discontent that the Tanzimat measures produced in some quarters, the financial shock after the Crimean War, the reaction against European-style manners among the upper class, and unease over the rights granted to non-Muslims by the Reform Edict of 1856 — but at the same time soldiers and officials who “had not been brought to the positions and offices they wanted”. Here too the label is not single: Namık Kemal interpreted the Kuleli Incident as a movement for liberty and found that the secret trial of the accused by the council of ministers was contrary to the legal principles of the Rescript of Gülhane. The TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam also writes that the movement later influenced the Young Ottomans and the Young Turks.\n\n⑤ 1909 · The Thirty-First of March Incident. 31 March 1325 in the Rumi calendar / 13 April 1909; it lasted eleven days, the Action Army took control of the city on 24 April, and on 27 April Abdülhamid II was deposed. The soldiers of the 4th Rifle (Avcı) Battalion who mutinied rose with a demand for the sharia; among the demands the rebels conveyed to parliament was also the full application of the provisions of the sharia. But the list of causes counted by the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam is far broader: excessive politicization in the army, the conflict between officers risen from the ranks and academy-trained officers and the purge of the former, the loss of privileges by the old cadres, a sweeping reorganization and reduction of the bureaucracy, the struggle for power between the Committee of Union and Progress and the Ahrar (Liberal) Party, the repressive attitude of the Unionists, and assassinations by unknown perpetrators. And the TDV Encyclopaedia of Islam makes the label itself a matter of debate: it was the Unionists who applied the description “reactionary uprising”; according to the article, in both Unionist and Islamist literature this description “has been seen as an instrument of the desire to bring back the era of despotism”, and “in our recent history the reactionary aspect of the 31 March Incident has been brought to the fore as more important than the incident itself”, serving as historical evidence for later politics. Moreover, Islamist figures of the period such as Elmalılı Muhammed Hamdi, Manastırlı İsmail Hakkı and Mehmed Akif openly declared that what had happened was unacceptable from the standpoint of Islam and that constitutional government could not be given up.\n\n📌 three patterns that emerge from the five cases — not a verdict, but an observation:\n· None has a single cause. In every case grievances over pay, arpalık, office and taxes are found within the same crowd as the complaint about innovation; it is often impossible to separate the two.\n· The crowd's reasons and the plotters' reasons may not be the same: in 1730 the grand vizier's son-in-law, in 1807 the deputy grand vizier and the Shaykh al-Islam, and in 1909 party struggle stand behind the scene.\n· It is most often the victor who has applied the label. The descriptions “reaction” in 1909 and “revolt against a reforming sultan” in 1622 come from sources that were themselves parties to the conflict. This does not mean that the uprisings were not opposed to innovation; it means that the description and the reasons must be read separately.", "5d0d6e5c"]},
"yirmisekiz-elciligin-onemi":{"baslik": ["Why Yirmisekiz Çelebi's embassy to Paris matters", "a6d4b16e"], "bag": ["Significance: TDV highlights this embassy because it regards it as a contribution to the development of Turkish-French relations and to the gradual erasing of the deep-rooted false image of the Turk in the West.", "da644279"], "metin": ["① A new job description. The instructions given to the envoy required him, apart from political business, to learn thoroughly about France's means of development and education and to report on those that could be applied. According to the article on the Tulip Era, the envoys of the period in Paris, Vienna and Moscow were likewise expected to gather information on European diplomacy and military power. The envoy was no longer only a bearer of letters but also an observer.\n\n② Repairing an image. At the reception of Müteferrika Süleyman Agha, sent to Louis XIV, problems of protocol had arisen, and at the king's wish scenes belittling the Turkish envoy had been put into Molière's comedy 'The Would-Be Gentleman'. According to TDV, Mehmed Efendi's embassy was of great importance in demolishing this negative image. He also played a major role in the spread of the 'Turquerie' fashion in Paris.\n\n③ The Sefâretnâme. It helped form the first knowledge of the West in the Ottoman lands. Assessments diverge: some say that he did not compare the two worlds; Tanpınar, however, writes that in its every line there is a hidden comparison and 'a whole programme'. TDV also notes that a connection can be drawn with the comparative outlook in Montesquieu's Persian Letters of the same years.\n\n④ Architecture. According to the Venetian ambassador Emo, the palaces and gardens in Istanbul were built according to the pictures and plans that Mehmed Efendi brought from Paris; the interpreter Lenoir had procured these plans for him. TDV says there can be no doubt that the Versailles pools and animal statues described in the Sefâretnâme served as models for the pools and public fountains (sebil) of Kâğıthane (see lale-devri-mimari-ve-sehir, mimari-sadabad).\n\n⑤ The printing press. According to TDV, one of the most important results of the embassy was the effort of his son Said Efendi to set up the first Turkish printing press together with İbrahim Müteferrika. Mehmed Efendi had told Saint-Simon, whom he came to know in Paris, that a printing press would soon be opened in Istanbul.\n\n⑥ The result that did not come about. The effort to form an offensive-defensive alliance with France against Austria came to nothing.", "4a343410"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-kabul-toreni":{"baslik": ["Before the twelve-year-old king: a painting, a medallion and a newspaper", "1a2f9de8"], "metin": ["According to TDV, the reception at the Tuileries Palace on 21 March 1721 turned into a ceremony the like of which had not been seen in Paris. The envoy presented the imperial letter of Ahmed III, İbrahim Pasha's letter and the gifts to the twelve-year-old Louis XV and spoke of the strengthening of friendship between the two states.\n\nITS TRACES. The famous pictures showing the envoy's entry into the palace from the Tuileries garden and his departure over the Swing Bridge after the audience bear the signature of the painter Charles Parrocel. A silver medallion was struck showing the reception, with a portrait of Louis XV on its reverse. The ceremony was reported in detail in the newspaper Le Nouveau Mercure.\n\nAFTERWARDS. The envoy was invited to the king's hunts, also visited the king privately (Marshal Villeroi was present) and had the opportunity to see the king's treasury.", "7d37be94"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-moliere":{"baslik": ["The 'Turkish envoy' on Molière's stage — and the embassy that made him forgotten", "b39c240b"], "metin": ["At the reception of Müteferrika Süleyman Agha, who went to Paris in the reign of Louis XIV, problems of protocol had arisen. According to TDV, at the king's request, scenes belittling the Turkish envoy were put into Molière's comedy 'Bourgeois gentilhomme' (The Would-Be Gentleman).\n\nThe entry into Paris of Yirmisekiz Çelebi Mehmed Efendi, who came half a century later, was magnificent, and he became the focus of public interest. TDV writes that this embassy was of great importance in demolishing that negative image.", "bb13ed6b"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-opera":{"baslik": ["An Ottoman envoy at the opera: Thésée and Omphale", "376e64fc"], "metin": ["According to TDV, the opera that left Mehmed Efendi amazed was the lyric tragedy 'Thésée', with a libretto by Quinault and music by Lully; he watched this performance at the palace together with the king. The opera he went to the second time was 'Omphale', with words by La Motte and music by Destouches. His son Said Efendi also watched the ballet 'Les Fêtes Vénitiennes'.\n\nThe envoy also toured the fortresses and fortifications of Paris.", "3329dc49"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-paris-sokaklari":{"baslik": ["Paris from the Sefâretnâme: seven-storey houses, crowded streets, islands in the Seine", "31d3cfba"], "metin": ["Observations summarized by TDV:\n• The city has 'wondrous and strange' buildings, palaces and gardens.\n• Houses are three or four storeys high; there are also very many houses built with seven storeys.\n• The city and its streets are crowded; the envoy attributes this to men and women being out in the street together.\n• The streets are wide and paved with paving stones.\n• The Seine runs through the middle of the city and forms three islands. Notre-Dame is also among the places he saw.\n\nINSTITUTIONS. In Paris he examined the house of physicians, the pharmacy (devahane), the observatory, the Gobelins tapestry works and the mirror factory.\n\nAccording to TDV, his describing Paris so extensively shows that he toured the city very thoroughly.", "72a849ea"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-said-efendi":{"baslik": ["The envoy's son who was lost for three days in the corridors of Versailles", "38325595"], "metin": ["The Duc d'Orléans's elderly mother, Princess Charlotte Elisabeth, did not meet the envoy, but according to TDV she took a close interest in the safety of the mission and especially in Said Efendi's health. For Said Efendi, who moved about quite independently of his father, had got lost in the corridors of Versailles together with a few women and reappeared only three days later.\n\nA few years later this same Said Efendi was to set up the first Turkish printing press together with İbrahim Müteferrika (see lale-devri-yenilikler).\n\nNOTE. TDV gives the incident as plain information but does not show its basis in this sentence; the article's bibliography includes the 1981 Paris edition expanded with French archival documents (Le paradis des infidèles).", "80993aea"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-versailles":{"baslik": ["A consolation in the face of Versailles: 'The world is the believer's prison and the unbeliever's paradise'", "82217276"], "metin": ["Mehmed Efendi toured the Duc d'Orléans's Château de Saint-Cloud and attended the dinner given in his honour. He then saw Versailles, the place he describes in the greatest detail in the Sefâretnâme, followed by the palaces of Trianon and Marly.\n\nAccording to TDV, he felt great admiration for the palaces, gardens, pools and fountains and described them in his own elegant style. He wrote that in the face of what he saw he found consolation in the subtlety of the saying 'The world is the prison of believers and the paradise of unbelievers'. He was aware that he had encountered a different culture; along with his admiration, he also took with him pictures and plans of the palaces and gardens.\n\nOUTCOME. TDV writes that the Versailles pools and animal statues in the Sefâretnâme served as models for the pools and public fountains at Kâğıthane (see mimari-sadabad).", "a3f64028"]},
"yirmisekiz-merak-yolculuk":{"baslik": ["From Istanbul to Paris: galleon, quarantine, canals", "bdec72c8"], "metin": ["The mission left Istanbul on 7 October 1720 aboard a merchant galleon assigned by the French ambassador and reached Toulon on the forty-sixth day.\n\nBecause there was an epidemic in Marseille, it was put into quarantine on an island near Montpellier. It then followed the road through Toulouse and Bordeaux, the canals of southwestern France and the river Garonne, and made its way to Paris by way of Orléans.\n\nTDV refers to this epidemic as 'cholera'; no separate source was read on the name of the disease.", "8c5f6ce2"]},
"zimmi-cizye-millet-duzeni":{"sebep.b": ["The fact that with the conquest of Istanbul the empire's most populous non-Muslim communities became subjects in a single center, and the need for an institutional framework to administer this population", "e3883d25"], "sonuc.b": ["The abolition of the jizya by the Reform Edict of 1856 (Islahat Fermanı) and the imposition of the obligation of military service on non-Muslims — the legal end of the four-century-old dhimma order", "117c6dd5"], "bag": ["The TDV article on the dhimmi (Zimmî) states that the classical order was “to a significant extent abolished after the Tanzimat”; the article on the jizya (Cizye) records that the Reform Edict of 1856 abolished the jizya and that it was replaced by the military exemption tax (bedel-i askerî), which lasted until 1907. According to the article on the millet, the Armenian millet had been established in 1461 when Mehmed the Conqueror appointed a patriarch in Istanbul; after 1856 lay elements entered communal administration and the powers of the patriarchates were reduced.", "9ca5302e"], "metin": ["A dhimmi is a non-Muslim who lives as a citizen in the abode of Islam and has been given a guarantee of protection: his life and property are inviolable, his worship is free, and he has the right to take disputes within his own community to his own court; in return he recognizes the sovereignty of the state and pays the jizya. The jizya was levied only on free, adult males who were able to work and able to pay — women, children, the elderly, the blind, the disabled, the poor and clergy who had withdrawn to a life of worship were exempt — and in the Ottoman state it was divided into three classes: âlâ, the highest (48 dirhams), evsat, the middle (24), and ednâ, the lowest (12). The communities were governed through the millet organization; this organization was based not on ethnic or linguistic affiliation but on religion and denomination, which is why Gregorian, Catholic and Protestant Armenians were counted as three separate millets. The patriarch and the chief rabbi collected taxes on behalf of the state, governed the community, applied the law and supervised education; but this authority was under the control of the state — in the 17th century alone the Greek Orthodox patriarch was changed fifty-eight times.", "4b065364"]}
});
